WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.980
It's been kind-of a rough couple days around here,

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.000
so today I want to try to cheer myself up by telling you

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.180
a story about this weird and beautiful moment in sports.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.720
Ok, so our story begins in 1984 at the finals of the European Cup,

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.120
which is this big soccer tournament where all the best

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.680
teams in Europe play each other. Today it's called the Champions League.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.940
And in 1984, the final game was between Roma and Liverpool.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.940
And at the end of the game it was tied 1-1, which meant there had to be a penalty shoot-out.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.680
At the time, Liverpool's goalkeeper was this guy named Bruce Grobbelaar,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.500
who was, even by goalie standards, a pretty eccentric dude.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.100
And for some reason, during this incredibly high-stakes

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.860
penalty shoot-out, he decided to feign wobbly-legged nervousness

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.980
as one of the Roma penalty-takers approached.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:48.180
And then that guy skied his penalty over the goal, and Liverpool won the Champions League

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.760
4-2 on penalties. And Bruce Grobbelaar cemented his status as a Liverpool legend.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.080
But, at the time this all happened in 1984, the hero of our story Jerzy Dudek

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.860
was a ten year old kid living in a coal mining town in Poland.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.940
And as a youngster, Jerzy Dudek was, like, a pretty good goalkeeper.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.460
In his teens and early twenties, he played for a third-tier Polish team.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.080
But as he got older, he became a really good goalkeeper. He found his way to Holland, and then eventually

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.160
to England, where he played for Liverpool beginning in 2001.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.200
Before we move on to the story, I just want to note that professional goalkeepers practice trying

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:26.260
to save penalties a lot. Like, by the time Jerzy Dudek arrived in England, he had tried to save

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.680
many thousands of penalties. When you're a goalkeeper, unless you're Bruce Grobbelaar,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.240
you learn your way of saving penalties. And for Jerzy Dudek, that was standing stock-still

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:40.520
and waiting until, like, a half-second before the kick came in and then jumping one way or another.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.580
Ok, so flash forward to 2005. Liverpool again find themselves in the finals of what is now called

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.580
the Champions League with a chance to win their first European Cup trophy since 1984,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.820
and Jerzy Dudek is the goalkeeper. Thereagain playing Italian opposition, this time A.C. Milan,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.360
and for Liverpool and Jerzy Dudek, the game starts horribly.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.500
At halftime, they are down three-nothing. And they weren't just losing, they were getting played off the pitch.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.620
Like, A.C. Milan were just a much better team. And then, essentially out of nothing, during one 6-minute stretch

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:16.320
in the second half, Liverpool score three goals to tie the game. And it remains tied for the next thirty minutes,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.260
and then through all thirty minutes of extra time, thanks, in no small part, to Jerzy Dudek.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:27.140
At the end of extra time, Dudek made a double save so incredible that, to this day, if you ask him about it,

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:32.280
he says that he does not remember making the save, and does not understand how he possibly could have made it.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.840
So, the game goes to a penalty kick shoot-out. You're Jerzy Dudek. You've been practicing saving penalties

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.760
since you were a kid. You have your way of doing it.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.920
You've done your research. You knew this was a possibility.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.820
You've laid awake at night imagining it: The Champions League final down to penalties, you in goal

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:54.020
standing stock-still until the moment before the ball is kicked like you always have.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:00.060
But then, one of your teammates Jamie Carragher walks up to you and says that in 1984, 21 years ago,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.540
this guy Bruce Grobbelaar did this weird "crazy legs" thing while defending penalties for Liverpool

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.920
in the European Cup final, and it worked! And Jamie Carragher is like, "You should do that!"

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.460
But that was in 1984, with different players, and a different coach, and a different opposition.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.900
What does that moment have to do with this one?

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.320
Quite a bit, as it turns out.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.420
There are times in your life when you do things precisely as you have prepared and practiced for them,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.720
and then there are times in your life when you listen to Jamie Carragher.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.920
So, in the most important moment of your professional life, you start dancing.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.740
You don't actually know how Bruce Grobbelaar did it, so you do your own version.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.340
And it works. Milan's first penalty-taker skies his shot over the goal,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:43.460
and you save two of the next four penalties, and Liverpool are European Champions once more.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.520
I think about this moment a lot, partly because I'm a Liverpool fan and we are notoriously

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:54.900
mired in our own history, but also because it reminds me that sometimes the right decision is the weird decision.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.440
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

